@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — examination: the corroborative test at the Zande poison oracle
#
# This protocol is unusual in the graph and the reason it exists: the
# falsifying condition was not devised here. The transmission states it, its
# own practitioners apply it, and the recorder printed two consultations in
# full so that a reader could count the outcomes himself. Nothing had to be
# constructed but the counting.
#
# What the protocol measures is narrow and is named as such below. It does not
# measure whether the oracle names the right man. It measures how often the
# transmission's own test of validity fails, in the only sample this graph has
# read at the wording.
#

pmc:BengeCorroborationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "The rate at which the corroborative test fails"@en, "Die Rate, mit der der Bestätigungstest scheitert"@de ;
    skos:definition "A count of the corroborated consultations printed in the source: how often the second test fell opposite to the first, as the transmission's rule of validity requires, and how often it fell alike and voided the verdict."@en ;
    skos:note "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the two seances printed, the first carries two corroborated pairs and both are recorded as contradictory: a question about taking on the taboos of vengeance against a question about the questioner's own survival under them, and a question about a man's safety in a named settlement against its explicit corroboration. The second seance carries one explicitly corroborative question, and it agreed; the recorder calls that seance a wholly successful consultation. Three pairs, two of them void by the transmission's own rule."@en ;
    skos:note "The count is the recorder's material and the counting is this graph's. He states outright that the seances are given so that the reader may judge for himself the proportion of fowls that die and the order of deaths and survivals, so the arithmetic is invited by the source and is not imposed on it."@en ;
    skos:note "Three pairs decide nothing about a rate and the number is entered so that a later reader can see how small it is. What it does establish is that the falsifying condition is not hypothetical: it occurred in two of the three cases this graph has actually read, and the source says elsewhere that the oracle frequently kills or spares both fowls."@en ;
    skos:note "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is from the transmission's own stated requirement rather than from a base rate of chance. A base rate is deliberately not entered: the poison is administered in a number of doses that varies with the size of the fowl and with the operator's reading of its earlier answers, so the probability that a given fowl dies is not fixed and no honest chance rate can be stated from what was read."@en ;
    skos:note "What the deviation is not. It is not evidence against the oracle in the transmission's own terms, because the transmission assigns every occurrence of the condition to one of eight causes that leave the instrument untouched. That is why the finding of this protocol is about the standing of the falsifying condition inside the transmission and not about the oracle's accuracy, which nothing read here measures."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination for why the undocumented case is treated as uncarried."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The two tests of one consultation fall alike, both fowls dying or both surviving, so that by the transmission's own rule the verdict is contradictory and void."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "The Azande, by the corroborative second test their own rule prescribes; the two consultations counted here were recorded and printed at length by E. E. Evans-Pritchard, who was present at both" ;
    pm:caseCount 3 ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), the two seances printed at pp. 141-143" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date .
